  <!-- Question 10: Hard - Robot Path Data Structure -->
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>S02.4.6: Robot Path Data Structure</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>In the robot path example, `robot_path` is a list of tuples. Why is this data structure a good choice?</p><pre><code>robot_path = [(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1)]</code></pre>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text>It allows the path to be extended (mutable list), while ensuring each (x, y) coordinate point is fixed and cannot be accidentally changed (immutable tuples).</text>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text>It is the only way to store 2D data in Python.</text>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text>The list is immutable, protecting the overall path, and the tuples are mutable, allowing coordinates to be updated.</text>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text>It is more memory efficient than using a list of lists for this task.</text>
    </answer>
  </question>

